   "title": "Rock garden (read me first)",
   "properties": {
    "text": ""
   },
   "widgets_values": [
    "Purely procedural modelling, no model files at all.\n\n① Left column: a rock library. For-Each (count mode) runs 8 times; each pass fills a small volume with a rock's signed distance field (the rock asset pipeline's: big broken faces with crisp arrises, chips, a flat bottom; rand_seed wired to iteration picks the shape, and seed % 4 the kind -- boulder, bedded slab, split shard, weathered block) and turns it into polygons. Match Size puts each rock's bottom on y = 0, Connectivity numbers each piece (class). Poly Reduce makes a low-poly set at 10% for the pebbles (LOD).\n\n② Second column: the ground as a height field (COP): a hybrid terrain fractal, a stream dug along a sine curve, then HeightField Erode -- rills down the slopes, scree at their feet, silt and gravel where the water slows -- and its masks colour the ground (soil on steep ground, scree on the debris, gravel along the stream, grass lush where water runs and dry on the spurs) and place the rocks: boulders on the scree and the gentle ground, pebbles on the stream banks. Height Field to Mesh turns it into a mesh with those as point attributes; Attribute Promote makes the weights face groups (the group field just says @boulder > 0.5).\n\n③ Third column: Scatter and Align scatters the big rocks (Avoid Overlap pushes them apart with Point Relax, Tilt keeps them from all sitting upright), a Wrangle picks a random class for each point, Copy to Points with Piece Attribute = class picks rocks by number - one node places all 8 kinds. Pebbles work the same way, using the low-poly library, no avoidance, more tilt.\n\n④ Rocks get a stone colour per kind and moss on the faces that point up, are merged with the terrain, and output a preview image, a 3D viewport and a glb."
   ],
   "color": "#432",
   "bgcolor": "#653"
  },
